{"id": "87742b97-3d85-4b98-838f-0d8130f0fea7", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T13:48:50.941872", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7697a9a7-d7ac-4779-a183-be1dfecc0e22", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "\n# Proposed Approach — Hierarchical vs. Non-Hierarchical Agents under Triggered Game Mechanics\n\n## 1. Repository inspection findings (evidence)\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file, `task.json`. Its embedded `documents.track.md` states: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- No game engine, agent framework, prior experiment code, environment wrapper, configuration files, logs, or dataset files are present.\n- System Python 3.12 is available; no RL/ML dependencies are pre-installed (`pip3 list` is empty). This is consistent with the design-only framing — none are needed for the design deliverable.\n- Implication for design honesty: every reference to a concrete game, engine, observation/action space, or trigger semantics is an *assumption*, not a repository observation. Assumptions and fixed requirements are separated explicitly below.\n\n## 2. Restating the research question in operational terms\nThe question asks whether activation of built-in game mechanics (illustrated by a pirate-invasion event that fires once an in-game score threshold is crossed) changes survival duration differentially for hierarchical vs. flat agents. This is naturally a 2×2 factorial question with an *interaction* as the primary quantity of interest.\n\n- Factor A — Agent control architecture\n  - H: Hierarchical — a high-level policy (manager / option selector / task decomposer) chooses among sub-policies; sub-policies emit primitive actions.\n  - F: Flat (non-hierarchical) — a single policy directly maps observations to primitive actions.\n- Factor B — Built-in mechanic activation\n  - M⁺: Mechanic active (default game behaviour; pirate invasion fires at/above a configurable score threshold).\n  - M⁻: Mechanic suppressed (control: trigger hard-disabled, all other state transitions and rewards intact — avoids a \"free-lunch\" confound).\n- Primary outcome: episodic survival duration (steps or in-game days until a terminating event).\n- Secondary outcomes: score at death, cause-of-death distribution, manager-option visitation, low-level action entropy, time-to-first-trigger.\n\n## 3. Conditions (4 cells; optional parametric sweep)\n| Cell | Architecture | Mechanic | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H / M⁺ | Hierarchical | Active | Primary experimental cell |\n| H / M⁻ | Hierarchical | Suppressed | Architecture-on / Trigger-off control |\n| F / M⁺ | Flat | Active | Architecture-off / Trigger-on control |\n| F / M⁻ | Flat | Suppressed | Baseline control |\n\nOptional sweep: vary the score threshold at ≥3 levels {low, mid, high} within M⁺ only, to test whether the interaction is monotonic.\n\n## 4. Consequential choices (please confirm before I lock these in)\n1. RL paradigm: both families trained with the same algorithm (PPO default). Identical optimiser hyperparameters, batch size, discount γ, GAE λ, entropy coefficient. Learning curves logged.\n2. Shared primitive action set / observation space between H and F, so the comparison isolates *control structure*, not representational power. The hierarchical agent's low-level policies must cover exactly the same primitive actions the flat policy could take; the manager only adds a commitment/commitment-duration layer above them.\n3. Mechanic-suppression control is \"matched\", not \"removed\": in M⁻ we keep score accumulation, scoring rewards, and all other state transitions; only the event-firing branch is short-circuited. This avoids the trivial confound where turning the mechanic off everywhere also reduces environmental hostility.\n4. Independent runs: each cell uses ≥30 independent random seeds (separate seeds for training and evaluation). Sample size justified a-priori to detect Cohen's d ≈ 0.5 at α=0.05, power=0.80 on log-survival.\n5. Statistical analysis: two-way ANOVA (architecture × mechanic) on log-transformed survival duration, with planned contrast on the interaction term. If normality fails, fall back to aligned-rank ANOVA; report partial η² and Cliff's δ with bootstrap 95% CIs.\n6. Train/eval split: train each architecture for a fixed compute budget (N environment steps); evaluation on held-out seeds, deterministic policy, no learning.\n7. Reproducibility: record seeds, library versions, hyperparameter manifests, evaluation traces; GPU-free compatible (gpu_enabled=false in task.json).\n\n## 5. Assumptions (clearly marked — replaceable if the researcher provides source)\n- A1. Game class: discrete-time, episodic survival-style game in which (a) the agent accumulates an in-game score/wealth metric, (b) crossing a threshold deterministically or stochastically triggers a built-in hostile event such as a pirate invasion, (c) explicit terminal conditions (death/capture/mission-end) define survival duration. If a specific title is intended, only the threshold value, trigger probability, and primitive action set need concrete numbers.\n- A2. Hierarchical policy family: two-level actor-critic with a manager that picks among k options/intents every τ steps; each option is realised by a low-level actor with its own termination condition (Sutton-Precup options, HIRO/Feudal sub-goals). \"Flat\" is a single actor-critic on primitive actions. Both share the same critic input.\n- A3. Mechanic semantics: score threshold is a scalar S_t compared against T; S_t ≥ T ⇒ event fires once per episode (or with configurable per-step probability). Suppression (M⁻) means the event branch never executes; S_t and other transitions are unaffected.\n- A4. Fairness priors: resource budgets (steps, wall-time, network capacity, replay buffer) are identical across cells. H gets extra parameters inside its manager, but training-step and sample budgets are matched (not parameter-count matched), which is the standard reporting in hierarchical-RL ablations.\n- A5. Evaluation metric: survival duration = number of environment steps from episode start to the first terminal event. If multiple deaths can occur, analysis uses time-to-first-terminal; ties censored at the maximum observed length.\n\n## 6. Feasible checks (proposed before reporting the headline interaction)\n- Smoke test: each cell runs ≥3 episodes with a random policy to confirm (i) episodes terminate, (ii) survival-duration variance is non-degenerate, (iii) suppression does not crash the engine.\n- Sanity on H vs. F alone (M⁻): in the no-mechanic control, hierarchical and flat should differ by less than the between-seed variability of either alone; a much larger gap signals an unfair low-level skill set and is corrected before the main run.\n- Trigger latency: verify that in M⁺ cells the event fires within a bounded window after the threshold is crossed; otherwise the threshold is mis-tuned for the score-acquisition rate.\n- No-leakage check: confirm M⁻ cells never experience the event across all eval episodes.\n\n## 7. What I will NOT do\n- I will not run training, evaluation, or any execution that would consume compute or produce a number. The deliverable for this turn is a written design only.\n- I will not invoke any learning algorithm or invent outcomes. Claims about expected results are framed as design-rationale predictions (e.g., \"if hierarchical abstraction helps re-plan under sudden threat, we would expect a positive mechanic×architecture interaction\"), not as findings.\n\n## 8. Uncertainties I would like the researcher to resolve (these change the design)\n1. Game / environment identity. Is there a specific game or benchmark in mind, or is a synthetic scenario acceptable?\n2. Hierarchical formalism. Options / feudal / manager-worker / sub-goal-conditioned — pick one so the ablation is well-defined.\n3. Score-threshold value(s). Single value or small sweep?\n4. Single- vs multi-agent universe. If multi-agent, do M⁺ events include cooperators or competitors, and do survival durations need to be defined per-agent?\n5. Termination semantics. Death-only vs. multiple terminal event types.\n6. Compute budget. Environment steps, wall-clock, or both (cpu_affinity_count=24 noted)?\n", "evidence": "\nRepository inspection (execute_check + inspect_repository):\n- /workspace contains exactly one file: task.json; embedded documents.track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"track.md\" 2>/dev/null returned no other tracks/projects anywhere on the filesystem.\n- pip3 list is empty in the system Python sandbox — consistent with design-only framing.\n- No game engine, agent code, configuration files, logs, or environment wrappers were located.\n\nTask configuration (task.json contents verbatim):\n- Question: \"Does the triggering of built-in game mechanics, such as pirate invasions when the score threshold is reached, affect the survival durations of hierarchical versus non-hierarchical agents?\"\n- Context: \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\"\n- Environment note: bubblewrap, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24.\n", "assumptions": ["Pirate invasions at a score threshold are an illustrative example of a built-in mechanic; the design should generalise to other triggered mechanics, with the threshold value being an experimental knob.</item>\n"], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-de7de677-2847-4df4-93c5-db6d11fe287c", "tool_call": {"id": "call-de7de677-2847-4df4-93c5-db6d11fe287c", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose plan for hierarchical-vs-flat agent survival experiment\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Proposed Approach \\u2014 Hierarchical vs. Non-Hierarchical Agents un