{"id": "c4943384-1462-475d-83e6-55f8bbe276fd", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T13:49:12.901767", "source": "user", "parent_id": "c313db1c-1d18-4e8a-8a79-7d4cbeef022f", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approved. Approach factorially sound hai aur assumptions/facts ka separation bhi clean hai. Brief remarks before main work shuru ho — phir full final experiment design deliver karein (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility, jaisa submission contract maangta hai): rationale hindi/english mixed chalega, lekin structure aur technical terms English mein rakhne better rahega clarity ke liye. Reminders jo mujhe chahiye final mein: (1) Architecture × Mechanic interaction primary hypothesis ke taur par explicitly state karein aur null hypothesis bhi likhein. (2) Mechanic-suppression ko 'matched' hi rakhein (sirf event-firing branch short-circuit, score aur baaki state transitions untouched) — yeh decisive hai confounds avoid karne ke liye, isliye design rational section mein justify karein. (3) Sample size ka justification Cohen's d≈0.5, α=0.05, power=0.80, log-survival pe a-priori explicit ho. (4) Statistical plan mein ANOVA + planned interaction contrast primary ho, aligned-rank ANOVA non-parametric fallback ho, aur Cliff's δ + bootstrap 95% CI reporting standard ho. (5) Compute budget ko do components mein quantify karein: training environment-steps per cell aur wall-time estimate (cpu_affinity_count=24 noted hai, gpu false hai). (6) Feasibility section mein smoke-test, no-leakage check, trigger-latency check aur M⁻ parity check ko gating criteria ke taur par list karein — yeh run karne se pehle ke failure modes catch karenge. (7) Hierarchical formalism: options (Sutton-Precup) ko default rakhein, lekin feudal/manager-worker/sub-goal-conditioned alternatives ko comparison table mein rakh dein taaki agar reviewer specific formalism prefer kare to trade-offs visible hon. (8) Sab numeric values (threshold T, manager cadence τ, k options, discount γ, GAE λ, entropy coef, training steps) ke beech 'assumed default, replaceable on source provision' clearly mark karein — yeh design-only task hai aur keys research-driven ya source-driven nahi hain, to transparency zaroori hai. (9) Expected outcomes section mein sirf design-rationale predictions likhein (e.g., 'positive interaction agar hierarchical abstraction re-planning enable karta hai sudden threat ke baad'); koi bhi claimed empirical result mat likhna — research question ka answer tabhi report hoga jab experiment sach mein run ho. Ab final design synthesis likhein jisme sections hon: research question operationalisation, hypotheses (H0/H1), conditions table, environment & mechanic semantics shared between cells, agent formalisms (H vs F), training procedure, evaluation procedure, statistical analysis plan, sample-size & power justification, feasibility & compute budget, risks & mitigations, reproducibility artefacts, aur explicit 'assumptions vs requirements' register. Execution, training numbers, ya simulated outcomes mat banaana.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "63c04773-3670-4a7d-b79e-ad25d01d540b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T13:50:46.628551", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c4943384-1462-475d-83e6-55f8bbe276fd", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "\n# Final Experiment Design — Hierarchical vs. Non-Hierarchical Agents under Triggered Game Mechanics\n\n> Scope reminder: this is a **design-only** deliverable. No training, evaluation, or empirical result is produced. Every number below marked `[assumed default, replaceable on source provision]` should be replaced by the researcher's chosen value once the concrete game/agent stack is fixed.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Repository & task observations (evidence block)\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json`. Its embedded `documents.track.md` says: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"track.md\"` returned no other project tracks; `pip3 list` is empty. No engine code, no agent framework, no prior experiment artefacts exist.\n- Task configuration: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, **gpu_enabled=false**, **cpu_affinity_count=24**, system Python 3.12.\n- Question (verbatim): \"Does the triggering of built-in game mechanics, such as pirate invasions when the score threshold is reached, affect the survival durations of hierarchical versus non-hierarchical agents?\"\n- Therefore every concrete value in this design is an *assumption*, not a repository finding. See §13 for the explicit register.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research-question operationalisation\nThe question maps to a 2 (Architecture = Hierarchical vs. Flat) × 2 (Mechanic = Active vs. Suppressed) factorial design, with **survival duration** of an agent in an episodic built-in-mechanic-activating game as the primary dependent variable, and an **interaction** as the primary quantity of interest (because the question asks whether the mechanic *affects* the two architectures *differently*).\n\n- Architecture (A): H / F.\n- Mechanic activation (M): M⁺ (default, score-threshold fires pirate-invasion event) / M⁻ (matched suppression).\n- Primary outcome (Y): episodic **survival duration** — defined in §5.\n- Secondary outcomes: score-at-death, cause-of-death distribution, manager-option visitation counts (H cells only), low-level action entropy, time-to-first-trigger.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hypotheses (H0 / H1) — primary focus on the A × M interaction\nPrimary statistical hypotheses (state explicitly):\n- H1 (alternative, primary): μ_{H,M⁺} − μ_{H,M⁻} ≠ μ_{F,M⁺} − μ_{F,M⁻}. The activation of the built-in mechanic **changes survival duration by a different amount** in hierarchical agents than in flat agents. Equivalently, the Architecture × Mechanic interaction term in a 2-way ANOVA on log-survival is non-zero. Direction is signed as a *design rationale prediction* in §14 (sign of the effect is not part of H1 here; it is reported in §14).\n- H0 (null, primary): μ_{H,M⁺} − μ_{H,M⁻} = μ_{F,M⁺} − μ_{F,M⁻}. The change in survival duration under mechanic activation is the same for both architectures (no A × M interaction).\n\nSecondary, exploratory (reported but not used for inference gating):\n- H1a: μ_{H,M⁻} ≈ μ_{F,M⁻} (architectures parity in absence of mechanic — a fairness check).\n- H1b: μ_{F,M⁺} < μ_{F,M⁻} (mechanic degrades flat-agent survival — a sanity check that the trigger is meaningful).\n- H1c: the mechanic's effect scales with threshold T in M⁺ cells (monotonicity of the threshold sweep, if invoked).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Conditions (cells of the factorial)\n| Cell | Architecture | Mechanic | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H / M⁺ | Hierarchical | Active | Primary experimental cell |\n| H / M⁻ | Hierarchical | Suppressed | Architecture-on / Trigger-off control |\n| F / M⁺ | Flat | Active | Architecture-off / Trigger-on control |\n| F / M⁻ | Flat | Suppressed | Baseline control |\n\nOptional threshold-sweep extension (only if compute budget allows — see §11): vary `T` at levels {low, mid, high} `[assumed default = {0.25·Q₅₀, 0.50·Q₅₀, 0.75·Q₅₀}]` within M⁺ cells only, where Q₅₀ is the median end-of-episode score observed in F / M⁻ baseline after §11's smoke test.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Environment & mechanic semantics — **shared between all cells**\n- Discrete-time, episodic simulator with explicit terminal conditions (death / capture / mission-end). Episode length cap: `L_max = 5000` steps `[assumed default]`.\n- Observation `o_t`: a feature vector including agent state, score/wealth `S_t`, threat level, local entities, and a 1-bit flag indicating whether the mechanic has fired this episode (only used for analysis, not observation policy input, to keep action space parity).\n- Action `a_t`: discrete primitive action set of size `|A|` `[assumed default |A| = 9]` shared by both architectures (move in 4 compass directions, attack, defend, gather, trade, idle, special). Same set across H and F.\n- Score / wealth `S_t` accumulates from positive game actions (gathering, trading, kills). It is **observable** and is the input to the trigger.\n- Trigger semantics (the \"pirate invasion\"):\n  - State machine boolean `fired_t` (False at t=0). Maintained identically in all cells.\n  - In **M⁺**: if `S_t ≥ T` and `¬fired_t`, fire event: spawn hostile NPC(s), raise local threat, alter transition dynamics. Set `fired_t` = True.\n  - In **M⁻**: check `S_t ≥ T` condition is still evaluated, `fired_t` is still tracked — but the event branch is **short-circuited**. `S_t`, score rewards, and all other state transitions remain identical to M⁺. This is the decisive confound-avoidance: `M⁻` is \"**the event never takes effect, but everything else the event would have touched**\" — not \"the mechanic and all its side-effects are removed\".\n  - `T` is fixed within a cell; only the threshold sweep (§3) varies it.\n- Per-step reward identical across cells (matched control extends to reward). Survival bonus `r_alive = +1` per step `[assumed default]`; mechanic-related negative reward in M⁺ (damage from hostile NPCs) is preserved.\n\nJustification for the matched-control design (rationale in mixed Hindi-English for readability): मूल खतरा यह है कि किसी भी built-in mechanic को पूरी तरह \"remove\" करने पर game balance पूरी तरह बदल जाता है — जैसे अगर pirate invasion branch हटा दें तो शायद score-cap, hostile spawn budget, या environmental hazard भी हट जाए, और हम architecture × mechanic interaction के बजाय architecture × environment-softness effect देख रहे होंगे। इसलिए `M⁻` का अर्थ है *\"event-firing branch ka short-circuit, baaki sab identical\"* — score untouched, transition dynamics untouched, reward untouched — ताकि `M⁺` vs `M⁻` का अंतर purely event-firing के causal effect को attribute करे। यही design decision interaction estimate को interpretable banata hai।\n\n---\n\n## 5. Agent formalisms: H (hierarchical) vs. F (flat)\n\n### 5.1 Default: H = Options / Sutton-Precup framework\n- Manager is a categorical policy `π_m(o_t) → i ∈ {1,…,k}` with options `[assumed default k = 4]` and a commitment duration sampled from a learned termination `β_i(o_t) ∈ [τ_min, τ_max]` with `τ_min = 5`, `τ_max = 50` `[assumed default]`.\n- Manager cadence: commits for an integer option-length `[assumed default τ = 20 steps]` before re-selecting (the lower-level option termination β can cut short early; manager policy can also force re-selection at cadence boundary).\n- Each option `i` is a low-level actor-critic `π_i(a_t | o_t, i)` with its own critic head; primitive action space is the shared `|A|` set.\n- Intra-option learning: every primitive step contributes to the active option's critic.\n- Manager cadence/termination is the only structural difference vs. F.\n\n### 5.2 Alternative hierarchical formalisms (comparison table — substituted on reviewer preference)\n| Formalism | Top-level primitive | Termination / commitment | Sub-policy input | Strength | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Options (Sutton-Precup, default)** | discrete option index `i` | learned β_i ∈ [τ_min, τ_max] | `o_t` plus option id | Simple, well-understood, off-policy friendly | Manager may learn \"sticky\" options |\n| **Feudal (Dayan & Hinton; FuN/Vezhnevets et al.)** | goal vector `g ∈ ℝ^{d_g}` | directed by manager periodicity | `o_t` plus residual goal | Spatial / compositional structure | Goal-representation design overhead |\n| **Manager-worker (two-level actor-critic; e.g., HAAR / Option-Critic)** | discrete skill id | learned termination per skill | `o_t` plus skill id | Direct credit assignment at each level | Risk of manager-worker staleness |\n| **Sub-goal-conditioned (HIRO / HAC)** | sub-goal in observation/goal space | learned | `o_t` plus sub-goal | Good for sparse-reward manipulation | Sub-goal space must be hand-designed |\n\nThe default (Options) is the formal choice; if the researcher prefers any row above, the four cells of the factorial remain isomorphic and the analysis plan is unchanged.\n\n### 5.3 F (flat, non-hierarchical)\n- Single actor-critic `π_f(a_t | o_t)` over the shared `|A|` primitive actions.\n- No manager; no commitment state.\n- Hyperparameters and network capacity matched to a single H low-level policy (not to the full H stack). The full-architecture advantage of H is therefore reported as *additional manager capacity*, not as a *total-parameter* advantage. This is the standard reporting convention in hierarchical-RL ablations.\n\n### 5.4 Fairness invariants across H and F\n- Same observation pre-processing, same primitive action set, same intrinsic reward shaping (none, except `r_alive`).\n- Same PPO hyperparameters (see §6).\n- Same training budget in environment-steps; same evaluator; same held-out seed pool.\n- Difference is **only** in control structure (manager layer vs. none).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Training procedure\n- Algorithm: **PPO** (Schulman et al., 2017) `[assumed default — replaceable on source]`. Actor-critic with shared backbone `64-64` MLP `[assumed default]`; activations ReLU; output heads differ (option index + β vs. primitive action + value).\n- Hyperparameters `[all assumed default, replaceable]`:\n  - Learning rate `3e-4`, Adam optimiser.\n  - Discount `γ = 0.99`, GAE `λ = 0.95`.\n  - PPO clip `ε = 0.2`, entropy coefficient `0.01`, value-loss coefficient `0.5`, max grad norm `0.5`.\n  - Rollout buffer: `N_steps = 2048` per environment `[assumed default]`.\n  - Mini-batches `8`, PPO epochs `4`.\n  - Parallel envs per worker: match to CPU budget (§11).\n- Training budget per cell (compute component 1): `5 × 10⁶` environment-steps per cell `[assumed default]`; sweep versions of M⁺ cells share 80% of this budget after warm-start from a common pre-training run (the shared pre-training reduces sweep over-budget and is documented as such).\n- Seeds: `n = 64` independent random seeds per cell `[assumed default — see §9 for a-priori justification]`. Seeds drawn from a master RNG with fixed seed; seed manifest published as a reproducibility artefact (§12).\n- Curriculum: none (we do not want curriculum to be a confound); all cells see the same difficulty-curve and identical episode randomness modulo trigger semantics.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evaluation procedure\n- Held-out evaluation seeds: `n_eval = 64` per cell (separate RNG stream; deterministic policy at evaluation).\n- Episodes: per evaluation seed, run `M = 30` episodes `[assumed default]`; report per-cell **mean and per-seed mean** (so each cell contributes `n_eval = 64` independent survival-duration samples to the ANOVA, not `30 × 64 = 1920`).\n- Deterministic policy at evaluation (mode of categorical heads, no sampling).\n- Episode termination captured as: `{death, capture, mission_end, timeout_at_L_max}`. Survival duration `D_e = min(t_death, L_max)`.\n- Per-cell logging: per-step (o_t, a_t, S_t, fired_t, r_t), episode summary {D_e, score at death, cause}, manager-option visitation histogram (H cells), low-level action histogram (all cells).\n- No learning during evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Statistical analysis plan\n- **Primary test:** two-way ANOVA on `log(D_e)` with factors `Architecture ∈ {H, F}` and `Mechanic ∈ {M⁺, M⁻}`. Use **planned interaction contrast** (the A × M interaction term) as the primary p-value, with α = 0.05.\n- **Pre-registered contrasts:**\n  1. Interaction contrast (primary).\n  2. Architecture main effect at M⁻ (parity check; §6 H1a).\n  3. Mechanic main effect at Flat (sanity that the trigger is meaningful; §6 H1b).\n- **Effect size reporting:** partial η² for the ANOVA; **Cliff's δ** for pairwise comparisons (H/M⁺ vs F/M⁺, etc.) with **bootstrap 95% CI** (10,000 resamples `[assumed default]`) on the log-survival difference.\n- **Non-parametric fallback:** if residual diagnostics on the log-survival distribution show non-normality (Shapiro-Wilk p < 0.01) or heteroscedasticity (Levene's p < 0.01), replace the ANOVA with an **Aligned-Rank Transform ANOVA** (ART-ANOVA; Wobbrock et al.) on rank-aligned `log(D_e)`; keep the same planned contrast structure. Effect-size reporting remains Cliff's δ + bootstrap CI.\n- **Multiple-comparison discipline:** interaction contrast is the single primary test; all other contrasts are at α = 0.05 with Bonferroni-Holm correction within the secondary family. No peeking at the threshold-sweep until the primary ANOVA is locked.\n- **Sweep analysis (if invoked):** A × T mixed-effects model with cell-aggregated log-survival, threshold as numeric fixed effect, architecture × threshold interaction; report whether the interaction slope differs from 0 with bootstrapped CI.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Sample-size & power justification (a-priori, on log-survival)\n- Target effect size: **Cohen's d ≈ 0.5** on log-survival (medium); α = 0.05 two-sided; power = 1 − β = 0.80.\n- For a two-sample comparison of equal-sized groups, the closed-form sample size is `n_per_group = 2 · ((z_{1−α/2} + z_{1−β}) / d)² = 2 · ((1.96 + 0.84) / 0.5)² ≈ 63.0`, round up to `n = 64` per cell `[assumed default]`.\n- For the 2 × 2 ANOVA interaction contrast, the same `n ≈ 63–64` per cell is sufficient (interaction contrasts in balanced 2 × 2 designs have approximately the same detectable standardized effect as a two-sample t-test of equal cell sizes; this is consistent with G*Power defaults for f = 0.25 four-cell design which yield total N ≈ 252 ≈ 64 per cell).\n- **Implication:** `N_total ≥ 4 × 64 = 256` evaluation-seed means across the four cells before the parametric sweep. If the sweep is added, multiply M⁺-cell counts by the number of T-levels (e.g., 3 levels × 64 = 192 in M⁺), with H and F still at 128 total.\n- Per-cell sample size is the primary driver; the **M = 30 episodes per evaluation seed** (§7) is for variance reduction of the per-seed mean and does not contribute independent observations to the ANOVA.\n- Sensitivity check: if observed effect size is smaller (d < 0.5), the design retains ~80% power down to d ≈ 0.5; below that, results are reported as exploratory with bootstrap CIs, without claiming inferential significance.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Feasibility: gating criteria (must pass before the full run)\nThese are run-before-the-main-experiment checks; failure of any one gates the main run for that cell.\n\n| Gating check | Pass criterion | If fail |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Smoke test** | With random policy, every cell runs ≥10 episodes to termination, `Var(D_e) > 0` (non-degenerate), and no exception is raised. | Fix environment wrapper; do not proceed. |\n| **No-leakage M⁻** | Across all `n_eval` M⁻ episodes, `fired_t` is observed True at the trigger evaluator but the event branch signal (e.g., hostile NPCs present) is observed False in 100% of those episodes. | Fix the suppression switch — most likely cause is a missed branch. |\n| **Trigger latency M⁺** | In M⁺ cells, given an evaluation episode reaches `S_t ≥ T`, the event fires within `[1, L_event]` steps `[L_event = 50, assumed default]`; ≥95% of qualifying episodes satisfy this. | Recompute `T` against the score-acquisition curve (use `Q₅₀` heuristic in §3) or adjust trigger semantics. |\n| **M⁻ parity** | All non-score, non-trigger game state variables (e.g., resource spawn rates, weather, neutral NPC behaviour) are observationally identical to M⁺ across at least 500 paired (seed, t-step) samples (paired t-test on matched states, p > 0.05 after Bonferroni). | Tighten the matched-control switch so M⁻ truly leaves everything else identical. |\n| **H–F sanity at M⁻** | Across random-policy rollouts in M⁻, the difference in mean `D_e` between H and F is < 1.5 σ of either's between-episode variance; essentially, F is not obviously broken. | Re-tune H primitives before main run. |\n\nStandard checks (not gating, but required): seed manifest reproducible; learning curves for both architectures show non-decreasing mean episode return up to training budget.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Compute budget — two quantified components (cpu_affinity_count = 24, gpu_enabled = false)\n**Component 1: training environment-steps per cell**\n- Base training budget: `5 × 10⁶` env-steps per cell `[assumed default, replaceable on source]` for the 2 × 2 factorial ⇒ `4 × 5 × 10⁶ = 2.0 × 10⁷` env-steps for the main factorial.\n- Each cell of the threshold sweep (3 levels × 2 architectures in M⁺) amortises 80% of steps via warm-start from a common pre-training run: `(3 − 1) × 5 × 10⁶ × 0.2 × 2 = 4.0 × 10⁶` extra env-steps for the sweep, for a total ≈ `2.4 × 10⁷` env-steps including sweep. **All values are placeholders; realistically justify once the real env step-rate is measured during smoke tests.**\n- Evaluation: `64 seeds × 30 episodes × L_max = 5000` ⇒ `~9.6 × 10⁶` evaluation steps per cell (cheap, no learning). Total eval ≈ `3.8 × 10⁷` steps.\n\n**Component 2: wall-time on 24 CPUs, no GPU**\n- Assumption `[assumed default, replaceable]`: a vectorised, CPU-friendly PPO loop in a lightweight discrete-time environment typically sustains `~500 – 2000 env-steps / sec / core` with 8 vectorised envs/core. We assume a conservative midpoint `s = 1000 steps / sec / core` until smoke test measures the real value.\n- Total env-steps (main + sweep + eval) ≈ `6.2 × 10⁷` ⇒ wall-time ≈ `6.2 × 10⁷ / (24 × 1000) ≈ 2583 s ≈ 43 min` for the entire study using all 24 cores continuously.\n- Two-hour buffer (safety factor ≈ 2.8× for variance in per-core throughput, sweep replications, and debugging) ⇒ design reserves up to **~2 hours wall-time** on the 24-core CPU-only node.\n- Should the smoke test measure `s < 250 steps / sec / core`, training budget is scaled down proportionally before booking compute; the analysis plan and sample size are unchanged because they are framed in env-steps.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Risks & mitigations\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Trigger fires too rarely in M⁺ to expose interaction | Medium | High (low power) | Tune `T` to match observed score-acquisition curve (use `Q₅₀` heuristic); §3 sweep. |\n| Trigger fires too often (every episode) | Low | Medium (saturated effect) | Raise `T` or shorten the score-acquisition tail via environment configuration; gating check §10 trigger-latency. |\n| Mechanical overfit in H (manager memorises trigger) | Medium | Medium | Held-out evaluation seeds; no training during eval; report learning curves for triggered and non-triggered sub-trajectories. |\n| Matched-control slip (M⁻ accidentally affects other state) | Medium | High (confound) | §10 M⁻ parity check is **gating** before main run. |\n| Learning-curve instability under PPO with options | Medium | Medium | Document hyperparameters; sweep entropy coefficient {0.005, 0.01, 0.05} as a small sensitivity check, reported but not used to chase an effect. |\n| Multiple-comparison inflation from sweep/secondary contrasts | Low | Low | Pre-registered contrast plan (§8); Bonferroni-Holm for secondaries; primary is the single interaction contrast. |\n| Sample-size shortfall at the interaction level | Low | Medium | A-priori `n = 64` per cell (§9); if attrition or seed failure cuts below 48, results are reported with bootstrap CIs only and labelled exploratory. |\n\n---\n\n## 13. Reproducibility artefacts\n- Seed manifest (training seeds × 64; evaluation seeds × 64; master RNG seed).\n- Hyperparameter manifest (JSON) — all values in §6 with their assumed-default flag.\n- Environment configuration (reward coefficients, score-acquisition curves, trigger threshold `T`, suppression switch).\n- Pre-registered analysis script per §8 (ANOVA + planned contrast; aligned-rank fallback; bootstrap CI computation).\n- Raw per-episode logs (compressed): (o_t, a_t, S_t, fired_t, r_t) plus episode summary.\n- Branch / version tag of the environment and agent code (commit id or git-describe equivalent).\n- Gating-check report (§10) signed off before main runs commence.\n- This design document itself archived as `design.md` next to those artefacts.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Assumptions vs. requirements register\nThis register records two columns and is the single source of truth for what is *fixed by the question / context* (requirements) versus what is *chosen by the design and replaceable once a real game / agent stack is provided* (assumptions). The editorial rule is: **never promote an assumption into a requirement silently; if a value changes, the impact on §9 sample size and §11 compute is recomputed.**\n\n**Requirements (fixed by the task statement / context)**\n- R1. Outcome is the effect of built-in mechanic triggering on **hierarchical vs. non-hierarchical agent** survival durations.\n- R2. Mechanic triggering must be **score-threshold based** (illustrated by pirate invasion at threshold).\n- R3. Both agent architectures must be present; an **interaction** is the question the design answers.\n- R4. Design-only deliverable; no experiments may be claimed as run.\n- R5. Network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores available; sandbox is bubblewrap.\n- R6. Researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic — i.e., the design must remain replaceable in concrete values without changing its structure.\n\n**Assumptions (replaceable on source provision — all marked `[assumed default]`)**\n- A1. Game class is a discrete-time, episodic simulator with explicit terminal conditions. `[replaceable]`\n- A2. Primitive action set size `|A| = 9`; observation is a moderately-sized feature vector. `[replaceable]`\n- A3. Episode cap `L_max = 5000` steps; per-step survival bonus `r_alive = +1`. `[replaceable]`\n- A4. Trigger threshold `T` is configurable; sweep levels {0.25 Q₅₀, 0.50 Q₅₀, 0.75 Q₅₀}. `[replaceable]`\n- A5. Hierarchical default = options (Sutton-Precup); alternatives are feudal / manager-worker / sub-goal; see §5.2. `[replaceable]`\n- A6. Manager cadence `τ = 20`, option count `k = 4`, `τ_min = 5`, `τ_max = 50`. `[replaceable]`\n- A7. RL algorithm = PPO; backbone = 64-64 MLP; learning rate `3e-4`; γ = 0.99; GAE λ = 0.95; clip ε = 0.2; entropy coef = 0.01; value-loss coef = 0.5; PPO epochs = 4; mini-batches = 8; rollout `N_steps = 2048`. `[replaceable]`\n- A8. Training budget `5 × 10⁶` env-steps per cell; sweep warm-start reuses 80% of the budget. `[replaceable]`\n- A9. Sample size per cell `n = 64` (Cohen's d = 0.5, α = 0.05, power = 0.80 on log-survival). `[replaceable — recompute if effect-size target changes]`\n- A10. Evaluation: `M = 30` episodes per evaluation seed, deterministic policy, no learning. `[replaceable]`\n- A11. CPU environment-step rate midpoint `s = 1000` env-steps / sec / core; design reserves up to ~2 hours wall-time on the 24-core CPU-only node. `[replaceable — recompute once smoke test measures real per-core throughput]`\n- A12. Bootstrap iterations = 10,000 for 95% CIs. `[replaceable]`\n- A13. Trigger event spans at most `L_event = 50` steps by default. `[replaceable]`\n- A14. Score *reward* is preserved in M⁻ (matched-control principle). This is a structural choice, not a parameter: see §4. `[structural — change only if the research question explicitly changes]`\n\n---\n\n## 15. Expected outcomes — design-rationale predictions only\nThis section lists **hypotheses about the form a result could take, derived from the design's structure, not from any empirical observation**. No result is invented; this is what the design is *set up to detect*, conditional on data.\n\n- If the A × M interaction contrast is positive on log-survival (i.e., `μ_{H,M⁺} − μ_{H,M⁻}` *less negative* than `μ_{F,M⁺} − μ_{F,M⁻}`), this would be consistent with a **manager-level reset-and-replan** mechanism: when the pirate invasion fires, a hierarchical agent can re-select a new option at its next manager cadence boundary, whereas a flat agent has no such re-planning keel; the alternative interpretations (e.g., the manager becoming a liability) would require the opposite sign and would be discussed alongside the result.\n- If the interaction is null, the design concludes that **the mechanic's effect on survival is architecturally invariant** under the chosen definition of hierarchical control and the matched-control M⁻. The null conclusion is reported with bootstrap CIs on Cliff's δ for the interaction contrast.\n- The threshold sweep (if invoked) would yield design-rationale predictions only: e.g., \"if mechanic-vs-architecture coupling is causal and not merely confounded by score-acquisition rate, we would expect the interaction magnitude to scale **non-trivially** with `T`\" — not a claim that it does scale. (No curve shape is predicted in advance.)\n- **Reminder of design-only contract:** whether the interaction is positive, negative, or null **is not decided here**. The answer to the research question becomes reportable only when this design is executed and §10 gating checks pass.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Summary of what this design guarantees\n- A pre-registered primary hypothesis (A × M interaction, with H0/H1 stated) and a single primary statistical test with pre-registered contrasts.\n- A matched-control M⁻ that removes only the event-firing branch — every other state transition and the score reward are preserved — so that `M⁺` vs `M⁻` isolates the event's causal effect on survival.\n- Honest, fully-replaceable numeric placeholders, isolated in §14 so that any reviewer-supplied real value (game, agent stack, hyperparameters) can be substituted without changing §1–§13's structure.\n- A feasibility gate (§10) that prevents the most common confounds (suppression leakage, futile thresholds, unfair H primitives) from contaminating the main run.\n- A two-component compute budget (§11) sized against the actual 24-CPU / no-GPU environment note in `task.json`.\n- Design-rationale-only expected outcomes (§15) consistent with the design-only contract.\n\n— end of design —\n", "evidence": "\nRepository inspection (execute_check + inspect_repository):\n- /workspace contains a single file: task.json. Embedded documents.track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"track.md\" returned no other tracks anywhere on the filesystem.\n- pip3 list is empty in the system Python sandbox; consistent with design-only framing.\n- No engine, agent framework, environment wrapper, prior run logs, or configuration files are present anywhere under /workspace.\n\nTask configuration (verbatim, from task.json):\n- Question: \"Does the triggering of built-in game mechanics, such as pirate invasions when the score threshold is reached, affect the survival durations of hierarchical versus non-hierarchical agents?\"\n- Context: \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\"\n- Environment: bubblewrap, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24, system Python 3.12.\n- The task spec lists 'documents': a single embedded track.md note. No other source files exist.\n\nDesign-rationale plumbing:\n- The 2×2 factorial (Architecture × Mechanic) is the canonical decomposition of the research question's verb structure (\"does X affect Y differentially for A vs B\").\n- The matched-control M⁻ design preserves score reward and all non-event state transitions §4, isolating the causal effect of the event branch.\n- Sample-size formula `n_per_group = 2·((z_{1−α/2}+z_{1−β})/d)²` with d=0.5, α=0.05, power=0.80 yields n≈63, rounded up to 64 per cell — standard for balanced 2×2 interaction contrasts.\n- All numeric thresholds, hyperparameters, and runtime numbers are sourced from common PPO defaults and marked [assumed default] in §14; they are explicitly NOT promoted to requirements.\n", "assumptions": ["Discrepancy between the task's \"design-only\" framing and the researcher's request for \"main work\" was resolved by interpreting \"main work\" as the full design synthesis (this document), not as experiment execution.</item>\n"], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-0c6ae13b-fdde-4dd0-96d8-3c842805b086", "tool_call": {"id": "call-0c6ae13b-fdde-4dd0-96d8-3c842805b086", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full final experiment design synthesis (result stage)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Final Experiment Design \\u2014 Hierarchical vs. Non-Hierarchical Agents under Triggered Game Mechanics\\n\\n> Scope reminder: this is a **design-only** deliverable. No training, evaluation, or empirical result is produced. Every number below marked `[assumed default, replaceable on source provision]` should be replaced by the researcher's chosen value once the concrete game/agent stack is fixed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Repository & task observations (evidence block)\\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json`. Its embedded `documents.track.md` says: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"track.md\\\"` returned no other project tracks; `pip3 list` is empty. No engine code, no agent framework, no prior experiment artefacts exist.\\n- Task configuration: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, **gpu_enabled=false**, **cpu_affinity_count=24**, system Python 3.12.\\n- Question (verbatim): \\\"Does the triggering of built-in game mechanics, such as pirate invasions when the score threshold is reached, affect the survival durations of hierarchical versus non-hierarchical agents?\\\"\\n- Therefore every concrete value in this design is an *assumption*, not a repository finding. See \\u00a713 for the explicit register.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research-question operationalisation\\nThe question maps to a 2 (Architecture = Hierarchical vs. Flat) \\u00d7 2 (Mechanic = Active vs. Suppressed) factorial design, with **survival duration** of an agent in an episodic built-in-mechanic-activating game as the primary dependent variable, and an **interaction** as the primary quantity of interest (because the question asks whether the mechanic *affects* the two architectures *differently*).\\n\\n- Architecture (A): H / F.\\n- Mechanic activation (M): M\\u207a (default, score-threshold fires pirate-invasion event) / M\\u207b (matched suppression).\\n- Primary outcome (Y): episodic **survival duration** \\u2014 defined in \\u00a75.\\n- Secondary outcomes: score-at-death, cause-of-death distribution, manager-option visitation counts (H cells only), low-level action entropy, time-to-first-trigger.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hypotheses (H0 / H1) \\u2014 primary focus on the A \\u00d7 M interaction\\nPrimary statistical hypotheses (state explicitly):\\n- H1 (alternative, primary): \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207b} \\u2260 \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207b}. The activation of the built-in mechanic **changes survival duration by a different amount** in hierarchical agents than in flat agents. Equivalently, the Architecture \\u00d7 Mechanic interaction term in a 2-way ANOVA on log-survival is non-zero. Direction is signed as a *design rationale prediction* in \\u00a714 (sign of the effect is not part of H1 here; it is reported in \\u00a714).\\n- H0 (null, primary): \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207b} = \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207b}. The change in survival duration under mechanic activation is the same for both architectures (no A \\u00d7 M interaction).\\n\\nSecondary, exploratory (reported but not used for inference gating):\\n- H1a: \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207b} \\u2248 \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207b} (architectures parity in absence of mechanic \\u2014 a fairness check).\\n- H1b: \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207a} < \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207b} (mechanic degrades flat-agent survival \\u2014 a sanity check that the trigger is meaningful).\\n- H1c: the mechanic's effect scales with threshold T in M\\u207a cells (monotonicity of the threshold sweep, if invoked).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Conditions (cells of the factorial)\\n| Cell | Architecture | Mechanic | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| H / M\\u207a | Hierarchical | Active | Primary experimental cell |\\n| H / M\\u207b | Hierarchical | Suppressed | Architecture-on / Trigger-off control |\\n| F / M\\u207a | Flat | Active | Architecture-off / Trigger-on control |\\n| F / M\\u207b | Flat | Suppressed | Baseline control |\\n\\nOptional threshold-sweep extension (only if compute budget allows \\u2014 see \\u00a711): vary `T` at levels {low, mid, high} `[assumed default = {0.25\\u00b7Q\\u2085\\u2080, 0.50\\u00b7Q\\u2085\\u2080, 0.75\\u00b7Q\\u2085\\u2080}]` within M\\u207a cells only, where Q\\u2085\\u2080 is the median end-of-episode score observed in F / M\\u207b baseline after \\u00a711's smoke test.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Environment & mechanic semantics \\u2014 **shared between all cells**\\n- Discrete-time, episodic simulator with explicit terminal conditions (death / capture / mission-end). Episode length cap: `L_max = 5000` steps `[assumed default]`.\\n- Observation `o_t`: a feature vector including agent state, score/wealth `S_t`, threat level, local entities, and a 1-bit flag indicating whether the mechanic has fired this episode (only used for analysis, not observation policy input, to keep action space parity).\\n- Action `a_t`: discrete primitive action set of size `|A|` `[assumed default |A| = 9]` shared by both architectures (move in 4 compass directions, attack, defend, gather, trade, idle, special). Same set across H and F.\\n- Score / wealth `S_t` accumulates from positive game actions (gathering, trading, kills). It is **observable** and is the input to the trigger.\\n- Trigger semantics (the \\\"pirate invasion\\\"):\\n  - State machine boolean `fired_t` (False at t=0). Maintained identically in all cells.\\n  - In **M\\u207a**: if `S_t \\u2265 T` and `\\u00acfired_t`, fire event: spawn hostile NPC(s), raise local threat, alter transition dynamics. Set `fired_t` = True.\\n  - In **M\\u207b**: check `S_t \\u2265 T` condition is still evaluated, `fired_t` is still tracked \\u2014 but the event branch is **short-circuited**. `S_t`, score rewards, and all other state transitions remain identical to M\\u207a. This is the decisive confound-avoidance: `M\\u207b` is \\\"**the event never takes effect, but everything else the event would have touched**\\\" \\u2014 not \\\"the mechanic and all its side-effects are removed\\\".\\n  - `T` is fixed within a cell; only the threshold sweep (\\u00a73) varies it.\\n- Per-step reward identical across cells (matched control extends to reward). Survival bonus `r_alive = +1` per step `[assumed default]`; mechanic-related negative reward in M\\u207a (damage from hostile NPCs) is preserved.\\n\\nJustification for the matched-control design (rationale in mixed Hindi-English for readability): \\u092e\\u0942\\u0932 \\u0916\\u0924\\u0930\\u093e \\u092f\\u0939 \\u0939\\u0948 \\u0915\\u093f \\u0915\\u093f\\u0938\\u0940 \\u092d\\u0940 built-in mechanic \\u0915\\u094b \\u092a\\u0942\\u0930\\u0940 \\u0924\\u0930\\u0939 \\\"remove\\\" \\u0915\\u0930\\u0928\\u0947 \\u092a\\u0930 game balance \\u092a\\u0942\\u0930\\u0940 \\u0924\\u0930\\u0939 \\u092c\\u0926\\u0932 \\u091c\\u093e\\u0924\\u093e \\u0939\\u0948 \\u2014 \\u091c\\u0948\\u0938\\u0947 \\u0905\\u0917\\u0930 pirate invasion branch \\u0939\\u091f\\u093e \\u0926\\u0947\\u0902 \\u0924\\u094b \\u0936\\u093e\\u092f\\u0926 score-cap, hostile spawn budget, \\u092f\\u093e environmental hazard \\u092d\\u0940 \\u0939\\u091f \\u091c\\u093e\\u090f, \\u0914\\u0930 \\u0939\\u092e architecture \\u00d7 mechanic interaction \\u0915\\u0947 \\u092c\\u091c\\u093e\\u092f architecture \\u00d7 environment-softness effect \\u0926\\u0947\\u0916 \\u0930\\u0939\\u0947 \\u0939\\u094b\\u0902\\u0917\\u0947\\u0964 \\u0907\\u0938\\u0932\\u093f\\u090f `M\\u207b` \\u0915\\u093e \\u0905\\u0930\\u094d\\u0925 \\u0939\\u0948 *\\\"event-firing branch ka short-circuit, baaki sab identical\\\"* \\u2014 score untouched, transition dynamics untouched, reward untouched \\u2014 \\u0924\\u093e\\u0915\\u093f `M\\u207a` vs `M\\u207b` \\u0915\\u093e \\u0905\\u0902\\u0924\\u0930 purely event-firing \\u0915\\u0947 causal effect \\u0915\\u094b attribute \\u0915\\u0930\\u0947\\u0964 \\u092f\\u0939\\u0940 design decision interaction estimate \\u0915\\u094b interpretable banata hai\\u0964\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Agent formalisms: H (hierarchical) vs. F (flat)\\n\\n### 5.1 Default: H = Options / Sutton-Precup framework\\n- Manager is a categorical policy `\\u03c0_m(o_t) \\u2192 i \\u2208 {1,\\u2026,k}` with options `[assumed default k = 4]` and a commitment duration sampled from a learned termination `\\u03b2_i(o_t) \\u2208 [\\u03c4_min, \\u03c4_max]` with `\\u03c4_min = 5`, `\\u03c4_max = 50` `[assumed default]`.\\n- Manager cadence: commits for an integer option-length `[assumed default \\u03c4 = 20 steps]` before re-selecting (the lower-level option termination \\u03b2 can cut short early; manager policy can also force re-selection at cadence boundary).\\n- Each option `i` is a low-level actor-critic `\\u03c0_i(a_t | o_t, i)` with its own critic head; primitive action space is the shared `|A|` set.\\n- Intra-option learning: every primitive step contributes to the active option's critic.\\n- Manager cadence/termination is the only structural difference vs. F.\\n\\n### 5.2 Alternative hierarchical formalisms (comparison table \\u2014 substituted on reviewer preference)\\n| Formalism | Top-level primitive | Termination / commitment | Sub-policy input | Strength | Risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Options (Sutton-Precup, default)** | discrete option index `i` | learned \\u03b2_i \\u2208 [\\u03c4_min, \\u03c4_max] | `o_t` plus option id | Simple, well-understood, off-policy friendly | Manager may learn \\\"sticky\\\" options |\\n| **Feudal (Dayan & Hinton; FuN/Vezhnevets et al.)** | goal vector `g \\u2208 \\u211d^{d_g}` | directed by manager periodicity | `o_t` plus residual goal | Spatial / compositional structure | Goal-representation design overhead |\\n| **Manager-worker (two-level actor-critic; e.g., HAAR / Option-Critic)** | discrete skill id | learned termination per skill | `o_t` plus skill id | Direct credit assignment at each level | Risk of manager-worker staleness |\\n| **Sub-goal-conditioned (HIRO / HAC)** | sub-goal in observation/goal space | learned | `o_t` plus sub-goal | Good for sparse-reward manipulation | Sub-goal space must be hand-designed |\\n\\nThe default (Options) is the formal choice; if the researcher prefers any row above, the four cells of the factorial remain isomorphic and the analysis plan is unchanged.\\n\\n### 5.3 F (flat, non-hierarchical)\\n- Single actor-critic `\\u03c0_f(a_t | o_t)` over the shared `|A|` primitive actions.\\n- No manager; no commitment state.\\n- Hyperparameters and network capacity matched to a single H low-level policy (not to the full H stack). The full-architecture advantage of H is therefore reported as *additional manager capacity*, not as a *total-parameter* advantage. This is the standard reporting convention in hierarchical-RL ablations.\\n\\n### 5.4 Fairness invariants across H and F\\n- Same observation pre-processing, same primitive action set, same intrinsic reward shaping (none, except `r_alive`).\\n- Same PPO hyperparameters (see \\u00a76).\\n- Same training budget in environment-steps; same evaluator; same held-out seed pool.\\n- Difference is **only** in control structure (manager layer vs. none).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Training procedure\\n- Algorithm: **PPO** (Schulman et al., 2017) `[assumed default \\u2014 replaceable on source]`. Actor-critic with shared backbone `64-64` MLP `[assumed default]`; activations ReLU; output heads differ (option index + \\u03b2 vs. primitive action + value).\\n- Hyperparameters `[all assumed default, replaceable]`:\\n  - Learning rate `3e-4`, Adam optimiser.\\n  - Discount `\\u03b3 = 0.99`, GAE `\\u03bb = 0.95`.\\n  - PPO clip `\\u03b5 = 0.2`, entropy coefficient `0.01`, value-loss coefficient `0.5`, max grad norm `0.5`.\\n  - Rollout buffer: `N_steps = 2048` per environment `[assumed default]`.\\n  - Mini-batches `8`, PPO epochs `4`.\\n  - Parallel envs per worker: match to CPU budget (\\u00a711).\\n- Training budget per cell (compute component 1): `5 \\u00d7 10\\u2076` environment-steps per cell `[assumed default]`; sweep versions of M\\u207a cells share 80% of this budget after warm-start from a common pre-training run (the shared pre-training reduces sweep over-budget and is documented as such).\\n- Seeds: `n = 64` independent random seeds per cell `[assumed default \\u2014 see \\u00a79 for a-priori justification]`. Seeds drawn from a master RNG with fixed seed; seed manifest published as a reproducibility artefact (\\u00a712).\\n- Curriculum: none (we do not want curriculum to be a confound); all cells see the same difficulty-curve and identical episode randomness modulo trigger semantics.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Evaluation procedure\\n- Held-out evaluation seeds: `n_eval = 64` per cell (separate RNG stream; deterministic policy at evaluation).\\n- Episodes: per evaluation seed, run `M = 30` episodes `[assumed default]`; report per-cell **mean and per-seed mean** (so each cell contributes `n_eval = 64` independent survival-duration samples to the ANOVA, not `30 \\u00d7 64 = 1920`).\\n- Deterministic policy at evaluation (mode of categorical heads, no sampling).\\n- Episode termination captured as: `{death, capture, mission_end, timeout_at_L_max}`. Survival duration `D_e = min(t_death, L_max)`.\\n- Per-cell logging: per-step (o_t, a_t, S_t, fired_t, r_t), episode summary {D_e, score at death, cause}, manager-option visitation histogram (H cells), low-level action histogram (all cells).\\n- No learning during evaluation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Statistical analysis plan\\n- **Primary test:** two-way ANOVA on `log(D_e)` with factors `Architecture \\u2208 {H, F}` and `Mechanic \\u2208 {M\\u207a, M\\u207b}`. Use **planned interaction contrast** (the A \\u00d7 M interaction term) as the primary p-value, with \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n- **Pre-registered contrasts:**\\n  1. Interaction contrast (primary).\\n  2. Architecture main effect at M\\u207b (parity check; \\u00a76 H1a).\\n  3. Mechanic main effect at Flat (sanity that the trigger is meaningful; \\u00a76 H1b).\\n- **Effect size reporting:** partial \\u03b7\\u00b2 for the ANOVA; **Cliff's \\u03b4** for pairwise comparisons (H/M\\u207a vs F/M\\u207a, etc.) with **bootstrap 95% CI** (10,000 resamples `[assumed default]`) on the log-survival difference.\\n- **Non-parametric fallback:** if residual diagnostics on the log-survival distribution show non-normality (Shapiro-Wilk p < 0.01) or heteroscedasticity (Levene's p < 0.01), replace the ANOVA with an **Aligned-Rank Transform ANOVA** (ART-ANOVA; Wobbrock et al.) on rank-aligned `log(D_e)`; keep the same planned contrast structure. Effect-size reporting remains Cliff's \\u03b4 + bootstrap CI.\\n- **Multiple-comparison discipline:** interaction contrast is the single primary test; all other contrasts are at \\u03b1 = 0.05 with Bonferroni-Holm correction within the secondary family. No peeking at the threshold-sweep until the primary ANOVA is locked.\\n- **Sweep analysis (if invoked):** A \\u00d7 T mixed-effects model with cell-aggregated log-survival, threshold as numeric fixed effect, architecture \\u00d7 threshold interaction; report whether the interaction slope differs from 0 with bootstrapped CI.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Sample-size & power justification (a-priori, on log-survival)\\n- Target effect size: **Cohen's d \\u2248 0.5** on log-survival (medium); \\u03b1 = 0.05 two-sided; power = 1 \\u2212 \\u03b2 = 0.80.\\n- For a two-sample comparison of equal-sized groups, the closed-form sample size is `n_per_group = 2 \\u00b7 ((z_{1\\u2212\\u03b1/2} + z_{1\\u2212\\u03b2}) / d)\\u00b2 = 2 \\u00b7 ((1.96 + 0.84) / 0.5)\\u00b2 \\u2248 63.0`, round up to `n = 64` per cell `[assumed default]`.\\n- For the 2 \\u00d7 2 ANOVA interaction contrast, the same `n \\u2248 63\\u201364` per cell is sufficient (interaction contrasts in balanced 2 \\u00d7 2 designs have approximately the same detectable standardized effect as a two-sample t-test of equal cell sizes; this is consistent with G*Power defaults for f = 0.25 four-cell design which yield total N \\u2248 252 \\u2248 64 per cell).\\n- **Implication:** `N_total \\u2265 4 \\u00d7 64 = 256` evaluation-seed means across the four cells before the parametric sweep. If the sweep is added, multiply M\\u207a-cell counts by the number of T-levels (e.g., 3 levels \\u00d7 64 = 192 in M\\u207a), with H and F still at 128 total.\\n- Per-cell sample size is the primary driver; the **M = 30 episodes per evaluation seed** (\\u00a77) is for variance reduction of the per-seed mean and does not contribute independent observations to the ANOVA.\\n- Sensitivity check: if observed effect size is smaller (d < 0.5), the design retains ~80% power down to d \\u2248 0.5; below that, results are reported as exploratory with bootstrap CIs, without claiming inferential significance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Feasibility: gating criteria (must pass before the full run)\\nThese are run-before-the-main-experiment checks; failure of any one gates the main run for that cell.\\n\\n| Gating check | Pass criterion | If fail |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Smoke test** | With random policy, every cell runs \\u226510 episodes to termination, `Var(D_e) > 0` (non-degenerate), and no exception is raised. | Fix environment wrapper; do not proceed. |\\n| **No-leakage M\\u207b** | Across all `n_eval` M\\u207b episodes, `fired_t` is observed True at the trigger evaluator but the event branch signal (e.g., hostile NPCs present) is observed False in 100% of those episodes. | Fix the suppression switch \\u2014 most likely cause is a missed branch. |\\n| **Trigger latency M\\u207a** | In M\\u207a cells, given an evaluation episode reaches `S_t \\u2265 T`, the event fires within `[1, L_event]` steps `[L_event = 50, assumed default]`; \\u226595% of qualifying episodes satisfy this. | Recompute `T` against the score-acquisition curve (use `Q\\u2085\\u2080` heuristic in \\u00a73) or adjust trigger semantics. |\\n| **M\\u207b parity** | All non-score, non-trigger game state variables (e.g., resource spawn rates, weather, neutral NPC behaviour) are observationally identical to M\\u207a across at least 500 paired (seed, t-step) samples (paired t-test on matched states, p > 0.05 after Bonferroni). | Tighten the matched-control switch so M\\u207b truly leaves everything else identical. |\\n| **H\\u2013F sanity at M\\u207b** | Across random-policy rollouts in M\\u207b, the difference in mean `D_e` between H and F is < 1.5 \\u03c3 of either's between-episode variance; essentially, F is not obviously broken. | Re-tune H primitives before main run. |\\n\\nStandard checks (not gating, but required): seed manifest reproducible; learning curves for both architectures show non-decreasing mean episode return up to training budget.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Compute budget \\u2014 two quantified components (cpu_affinity_count = 24, gpu_enabled = false)\\n**Component 1: training environment-steps per cell**\\n- Base training budget: `5 \\u00d7 10\\u2076` env-steps per cell `[assumed default, replaceable on source]` for the 2 \\u00d7 2 factorial \\u21d2 `4 \\u00d7 5 \\u00d7 10\\u2076 = 2.0 \\u00d7 10\\u2077` env-steps for the main factorial.\\n- Each cell of the threshold sweep (3 levels \\u00d7 2 architectures in M\\u207a) amortises 80% of steps via warm-start from a common pre-training run: `(3 \\u2212 1) \\u00d7 5 \\u00d7 10\\u2076 \\u00d7 0.2 \\u00d7 2 = 4.0 \\u00d7 10\\u2076` extra env-steps for the sweep, for a total \\u2248 `2.4 \\u00d7 10\\u2077` env-steps including sweep. **All values are placeholders; realistically justify once the real env step-rate is measured during smoke tests.**\\n- Evaluation: `64 seeds \\u00d7 30 episodes \\u00d7 L_max = 5000` \\u21d2 `~9.6 \\u00d7 10\\u2076` evaluation steps per cell (cheap, no learning). Total eval \\u2248 `3.8 \\u00d7 10\\u2077` steps.\\n\\n**Component 2: wall-time on 24 CPUs, no GPU**\\n- Assumption `[assumed default, replaceable]`: a vectorised, CPU-friendly PPO loop in a lightweight discrete-time environment typically sustains `~500 \\u2013 2000 env-steps / sec / core` with 8 vectorised envs/core. We assume a conservative midpoint `s = 1000 steps / sec / core` until smoke test measures the real value.\\n- Total env-steps (main + sweep + eval) \\u2248 `6.2 \\u00d7 10\\u2077` \\u21d2 wall-time \\u2248 `6.2 \\u00d7 10\\u2077 / (24 \\u00d7 1000) \\u2248 2583 s \\u2248 43 min` for the entire study using all 24 cores continuously.\\n- Two-hour buffer (safety factor \\u2248 2.8\\u00d7 for variance in per-core throughput, sweep replications, and debugging) \\u21d2 design reserves up to **~2 hours wall-time** on the 24-core CPU-only node.\\n- Should the smoke test measure `s < 250 steps / sec / core`, training budget is scaled down proportionally before booking compute; the analysis plan and sample size are unchanged because they are framed in env-steps.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Risks & mitigations\\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Trigger fires too rarely in M\\u207a to expose interaction | Medium | High (low power) | Tune `T` to match observed score-acquisition curve (use `Q\\u2085\\u2080` heuristic); \\u00a73 sweep. |\\n| Trigger fires too often (every episode) | Low | Medium (saturated effect) | Raise `T` or shorten the score-acquisition tail via environment configuration; gating check \\u00a710 trigger-latency. |\\n| Mechanical overfit in H (manager memorises trigger) | Medium | Medium | Held-out evaluation seeds; no training during eval; report learning curves for triggered and non-triggered sub-trajectories. |\\n| Matched-control slip (M\\u207b accidentally affects other state) | Medium | High (confound) | \\u00a710 M\\u207b parity check is **gating** before main run. |\\n| Learning-curve instability under PPO with options | Medium | Medium | Document hyperparameters; sweep entropy coefficient {0.005, 0.01, 0.05} as a small sensitivity check, reported but not used to chase an effect. |\\n| Multiple-comparison inflation from sweep/secondary contrasts | Low | Low | Pre-registered contrast plan (\\u00a78); Bonferroni-Holm for secondaries; primary is the single interaction contrast. |\\n| Sample-size shortfall at the interaction level | Low | Medium | A-priori `n = 64` per cell (\\u00a79); if attrition or seed failure cuts below 48, results are reported with bootstrap CIs only and labelled exploratory. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Reproducibility artefacts\\n- Seed manifest (training seeds \\u00d7 64; evaluation seeds \\u00d7 64; master RNG seed).\\n- Hyperparameter manifest (JSON) \\u2014 all values in \\u00a76 with their assumed-default flag.\\n- Environment configuration (reward coefficients, score-acquisition curves, trigger threshold `T`, suppression switch).\\n- Pre-registered analysis script per \\u00a78 (ANOVA + planned contrast; aligned-rank fallback; bootstrap CI computation).\\n- Raw per-episode logs (compressed): (o_t, a_t, S_t, fired_t, r_t) plus episode summary.\\n- Branch / version tag of the environment and agent code (commit id or git-describe equivalent).\\n- Gating-check report (\\u00a710) signed off before main runs commence.\\n- This design document itself archived as `design.md` next to those artefacts.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Assumptions vs. requirements register\\nThis register records two columns and is the single source of truth for what is *fixed by the question / context* (requirements) versus what is *chosen by the design and replaceable once a real game / agent stack is provided* (assumptions). The editorial rule is: **never promote an assumption into a requirement silently; if a value changes, the impact on \\u00a79 sample size and \\u00a711 compute is recomputed.**\\n\\n**Requirements (fixed by the task statement / context)**\\n- R1. Outcome is the effect of built-in mechanic triggering on **hierarchical vs. non-hierarchical agent** survival durations.\\n- R2. Mechanic triggering must be **score-threshold based** (illustrated by pirate invasion at threshold).\\n- R3. Both agent architectures must be present; an **interaction** is the question the design answers.\\n- R4. Design-only deliverable; no experiments may be claimed as run.\\n- R5. Network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores available; sandbox is bubblewrap.\\n- R6. Researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic \\u2014 i.e., the design must remain replaceable in concrete values without changing its structure.\\n\\n**Assumptions (replaceable on source provision \\u2014 all marked `[assumed default]`)**\\n- A1. Game class is a discrete-time, episodic simulator with explicit terminal conditions. `[replaceable]`\\n- A2. Primitive action set size `|A| = 9`; observation is a moderately-sized feature vector. `[replaceable]`\\n- A3. Episode cap `L_max = 5000` steps; per-step survival bonus `r_alive = +1`. `[replaceable]`\\n- A4. Trigger threshold `T` is configurable; sweep levels {0.25 Q\\u2085\\u2080, 0.50 Q\\u2085\\u2080, 0.75 Q\\u2085\\u2080}. `[replaceable]`\\n- A5. Hierarchical default = options (Sutton-Precup); alternatives are feudal / manager-worker / sub-goal; see \\u00a75.2. `[replaceable]`\\n- A6. Manager cadence `\\u03c4 = 20`, option count `k = 4`, `\\u03c4_min = 5`, `\\u03c4_max = 50`. `[replaceable]`\\n- A7. RL algorithm = PPO; backbone = 64-64 MLP; learning rate `3e-4`; \\u03b3 = 0.99; GAE \\u03bb = 0.95; clip \\u03b5 = 0.2; entropy coef = 0.01; value-loss coef = 0.5; PPO epochs = 4; mini-batches = 8; rollout `N_steps = 2048`. `[replaceable]`\\n- A8. Training budget `5 \\u00d7 10\\u2076` env-steps per cell; sweep warm-start reuses 80% of the budget. `[replaceable]`\\n- A9. Sample size per cell `n = 64` (Cohen's d = 0.5, \\u03b1 = 0.05, power = 0.80 on log-survival). `[replaceable \\u2014 recompute if effect-size target changes]`\\n- A10. Evaluation: `M = 30` episodes per evaluation seed, deterministic policy, no learning. `[replaceable]`\\n- A11. CPU environment-step rate midpoint `s = 1000` env-steps / sec / core; design reserves up to ~2 hours wall-time on the 24-core CPU-only node. `[replaceable \\u2014 recompute once smoke test measures real per-core throughput]`\\n- A12. Bootstrap iterations = 10,000 for 95% CIs. `[replaceable]`\\n- A13. Trigger event spans at most `L_event = 50` steps by default. `[replaceable]`\\n- A14. Score *reward* is preserved in M\\u207b (matched-control principle). This is a structural choice, not a parameter: see \\u00a74. `[structural \\u2014 change only if the research question explicitly changes]`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Expected outcomes \\u2014 design-rationale predictions only\\nThis section lists **hypotheses about the form a result could take, derived from the design's structure, not from any empirical observation**. No result is invented; this is what the design is *set up to detect*, conditional on data.\\n\\n- If the A \\u00d7 M interaction contrast is positive on log-survival (i.e., `\\u03bc_{H,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{H,M\\u207b}` *less negative* than `\\u03bc_{F,M\\u207a} \\u2212 \\u03bc_{F,M\\u207b}`), this would be consistent with a **manager-level reset-and-replan** mechanism: when the pirate invasion fires, a hierarchical agent can re-select a new option at its next manager cadence boundary, whereas a flat agent has no such re-planning keel; the alternative interpretations (e.g., the manager becoming a liability) would require the opposite sign and would be discussed alongside the result.\\n- If the interaction is null, the design concludes that **the mechanic's effect on survival is architecturally invariant** under the chosen definition of hierarchical control and the matched-control M\\u207b. The null conclusion is reported with bootstrap CIs on Cliff's \\u03b4 for the interaction contrast.\\n- The threshold sweep (if invoked) would yield design-rationale predictions only: e.g., \\\"if mechanic-vs-architecture coupling is causal and not merely confounded by score-acquisition rate, we would expect the interaction magnitude to scale **non-trivially** with `T`\\\" \\u2014 not a claim that it does scale. (No curve shape is predicted in advance.)\\n- **Reminder of design-only contract:** whether the interaction is positive, negative, or null **is not decided here**. The answer to the research question becomes reportable only when this design is executed and \\u00a710 gating checks pass.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. Summary of what this design guarantees\\n- A pre-registered primary hypothesis (A \\u00d7 M interaction, with H0/H1 stated) and a single primary statistical test with pre-registered contrasts.\\n- A matched-control M\\u207b that removes only the event-firing branch \\u2014 every other state transition and the score reward are preserved \\u2014 so that `M\\u207a` vs `M\\u207b` isolates the event's causal effect on survival.\\n- Honest, fully-replaceable numeric placeholders, isolated in \\u00a714 so that any reviewer-supplied real value (game, agent stack, hyperparameters) can be substituted without changing \\u00a71\\u2013\\u00a713's structure.\\n- A feasibility gate (\\u00a710) that prevents the most common confounds (suppression leakage, futile thresholds, unfair H primitives) from contaminating the main run.\\n- A two-component compute budget (\\u00a711) sized against the actual 24-CPU / no-GPU environment note in `task.json`.\\n- Design-rationale-only expected outcomes (\\u00a715) consistent with the design-only contract.\\n\\n\\u2014 end of design \\u2014\\n\", \"evidence\": \"\\nRepository inspection (execute_check + inspect_repository):\\n- /workspace contains a single file: task.json. Embedded documents.track.md states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"track.md\\\" returned no other tracks anywhere on the filesystem.\\n- pip3 list is empty in the system Python sandbox; consistent with design-only framing.\\n- No engine, agent framework, environment wrapper, prior run logs, or configuration files are present anywhere under /workspace.\\n\\nTask configuration (verbatim, from task.json):\\n- Question: \\\"Does the triggering of built-in game mechanics, such as pirate invasions when the score threshold is reached, affect the survival durations of hierarchical versus non-hierarchical agents?\\\"\\n- Context: \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\"\\n- Environment: bubblewrap, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24, system Python 3.12.\\n- The task spec lists 'documents': a single embedded track.md note. No other source files exist.\\n\\nDesign-rationale plumbing:\\n- The 2\\u00d72 factorial (Architecture \\u00d7 Mechanic) is the canonical decomposition of the research question's verb structure (\\\"does X affect Y differentially for A vs B\\\").\\n- The matched-control M\\u207b design preserves score reward and all non-event state transitions \\u00a74, isolating the causal effect of the event branch.\\n- Sample-size formula `n_per_group = 2\\u00b7((z_{1\\u2212\\u03b1/2}+z_{1\\u2212\\u03b2})/d)\\u00b2` with d=0.5, \\u03b1=0.05, power=0.80 yields n\\u224863, rounded up to 64 per cell \\u2014 standard for balanced 2\\u00d72 interaction contrasts.\\n- All numeric thresholds, hyperparameters, and runtime numbers are sourced from common PPO defaults and marked [assumed default] in \\u00a714; they are explicitly NOT promoted to requirements.\\n\", \"assumptions\": [\"Discrepancy between the task's \\\"design-only\\\" framing and the researcher's request for \\\"main work\\\" was resolved by interpreting \\\"main work\\\" as the full design synthesis (this document), not as experiment execution.</item>\\n\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-76fec76a-1bc8-4e7a-8e7b-7dcb2f951e1a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
